      "seed": 1072606198,
      "version": 1349,
      "versionNonce": 782104502,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1793353978112,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Design SmartThings app\n\n1. Functional Requirements : \n    1. Device Discovery & Identity: Standardized resources must be exposed so devices can identify themselves (e.g., /oic/id\nand /oic/p for hardware info).\n    2. Remote Control & Connectivity: Users must be able to control devices securely over the cloud, seamlessly traversing home\nfirewalls and NATs.\n    3. Real-time Notifications: Devices must push state changes proactively to the cloud without relying on heavy HTTP polling.\n    4. Connection Liveness: The system must definitively know if a device is online or offline, as hardware power loss or Wi-Fi\ncrashes won't reliably trigger OS-level TCP disconnects.\n    5. Multi-Vendor Support (UCI): The architecture must support Universal Cloud Interfaces to allow cross-cloud control (e.g.,\ncontrolling a Samsung device via a Google Home cloud).\n\n2. Non-Functional Requirement : \n    1. Extreme Connection Scale : Support upto 30 M concurrent long - lived connections\n    2. High-Throughput and Low latency : handle upto 10K TPS with lightning fast binary parsing\n    3. Bandwidth optimization : Operate flawless on highly constrained embedded devices\n    4. Resilliance against offline devices : Devices unreachability must never cause cascading thread pool exhaustion in backend.\n    5. High availability and eventual consistency for high throughput data paths\n\n3. Back of the envelope estimation : \n    Scale : 30M active IoT devices : 1M DAU\n    Ping : If 30M devices send application layer pings every 3 minutes to keep NAT tunnel opens => 30 M / 3 * 60 =166000 QPS\n    Peak payload traffic of 10,000 TPS\n    Storage : Assuming 1KB metadata => 30M * 1KB = 30 GB hot storage\n\n4. API designing : \n    1. Read State: GET /api/v1/devices/{id}?if=oic.if.rw. By forcing the Read-Write interface (oic.if.rw), the system strips\nheavy metadata out of the payload, reducing packet size by 60%.\n    2. Batch Updates: POST /api/v1/devices/batch?if=oic.if.ll. Uses the Link List (oic.if.ll) interface to control multiple\nresources in one high-TPS request.\n\n5. Data models and Entities : \n    1. Redis Cluster (Hot Presence & State Cache):\n        i). Configuration registery - device:{device_id}:location -> Value: gateway_server_id (Tracks which specific backend node\nholds the device's TCP socket).   \n        ii). HSET to store the latest telemetry. Key: state:device:{device_id} -> Fields: temperature: 22, switch: true. This\nallows the event processor to update a single sensor reading in O(1) time without fetching or rewriting the entire device\npayload.\n        iii). SET ... NX (Set if Not Exists) to handle duplicate commands from users on shaky mobile networks.   Key:\nidempotency:cmd:{command_id} -> Value: status.If the key already exists, the NX operation fails, allowing you to instantly drop\nthe duplicate request before it travels down the TCP tunnel.\n   \n    2. PostgreSQL (Metadata & Registration):devices table: id, user_id, manufacturer (mnmn), vendor_id (vid), resource_type\n(rt).   \n    3. Cassandra / S3: Cold storage for massive time-series telemetry data and historical device events. \n\n6. High Level Design : ",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Design SmartThings app\n\n1. Functional Requirements : \n    1. Device Discovery & Identity: Standardized resources must be exposed so devices can identify themselves (e.g., /oic/id and /oic/p for hardware info).\n    2. Remote Control & Connectivity: Users must be able to control devices securely over the cloud, seamlessly traversing home firewalls and NATs.\n    3. Real-time Notifications: Devices must push state changes proactively to the cloud without relying on heavy HTTP polling.\n    4. Connection Liveness: The system must definitively know if a device is online or offline, as hardware power loss or Wi-Fi crashes won't reliably trigger OS-level TCP disconnects.\n    5. Multi-Vendor Support (UCI): The architecture must support Universal Cloud Interfaces to allow cross-cloud control (e.g., controlling a Samsung device via a Google Home cloud).\n\n2. Non-Functional Requirement : \n    1. Extreme Connection Scale : Support upto 30 M concurrent long - lived connections\n    2. High-Throughput and Low latency : handle upto 10K TPS with lightning fast binary parsing\n    3. Bandwidth optimization : Operate flawless on highly constrained embedded devices\n    4. Resilliance against offline devices : Devices unreachability must never cause cascading thread pool exhaustion in backend.\n    5. High availability and eventual consistency for high throughput data paths\n\n3. Back of the envelope estimation : \n    Scale : 30M active IoT devices : 1M DAU\n    Ping : If 30M devices send application layer pings every 3 minutes to keep NAT tunnel opens => 30 M / 3 * 60 =166000 QPS\n    Peak payload traffic of 10,000 TPS\n    Storage : Assuming 1KB metadata => 30M * 1KB = 30 GB hot storage\n\n4. API designing : \n    1. Read State: GET /api/v1/devices/{id}?if=oic.if.rw. By forcing the Read-Write interface (oic.if.rw), the system strips heavy metadata out of the payload, reducing packet size by 60%.\n    2. Batch Updates: POST /api/v1/devices/batch?if=oic.if.ll. Uses the Link List (oic.if.ll) interface to control multiple resources in one high-TPS request.\n\n5. Data models and Entities : \n    1. Redis Cluster (Hot Presence & State Cache):\n        i). Configuration registery - device:{device_id}:location -> Value: gateway_server_id (Tracks which specific backend node holds the device's TCP socket).   \n        ii). HSET to store the latest telemetry. Key: state:device:{device_id} -> Fields: temperature: 22, switch: true. This allows the event processor to update a single sensor reading in O(1) time without fetching or rewriting the entire device payload.\n        iii). SET ... NX (Set if Not Exists) to handle duplicate commands from users on shaky mobile networks.   Key: idempotency:cmd:{command_id} -> Value: status.If the key already exists, the NX operation fails, allowing you to instantly drop the duplicate request before it travels down the TCP tunnel.\n   \n    2. PostgreSQL (Metadata & Registration):devices table: id, user_id, manufacturer (mnmn), vendor_id (vid), resource_type (rt).   \n    3. Cassandra / S3: Cold storage for massive time-series telemetry data and historical device events. \n\n6. High Level Design : ",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
